        setup: {
          players: 'Exactly 4 players, each playing for themselves.',
          deck: 'One double-six domino set (28 tiles).',
          equipment: '',
          dealing: 'Each player receives 7 tiles, leaving no boneyard.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Avoid accumulating points. The player with the lowest total score after 7 deals wins; the player with the highest score is the loser.',
          gameplay: '1. Matching: Play proceeds counter-clockwise. Tiles are played in a single line, matching the pips on the exposed ends.\n2. Going Out: A deal ends when a player plays their last tile.\n3. Scoring: When a player goes out, all other players sum the pips on their remaining tiles and add them to their cumulative score.',
          keyRules: [
            'Secret Scoring: The official scorer should keep the cumulative totals hidden until the final deal to maintain suspense.',
            "Social Stake: The loser is traditionally responsible for providing 'social refreshments' (beer) for the group.",
            'Winning condition: Avoid accumulating points.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Dump your 'heavies' early. High doublendtiles (6-6, 5-5) are a massive liability in Gallinazo. If you can't play them early, they will balloon your score if the deal ends suddenly.",
          intermediate: "Pip Management: Toward the end of a deal, try to keep tiles that give you the most matching options (like a 1nd1 or 2nd2) rather than tiles with high pips. A low-value 'dead end' tile can be used to block the game if you are sure you have the lowest score.",
          advanced: 'The Block Trap: If you have a very low pip count (e.g., only a 0-1 and 1nd1 left), you may want to deliberately block the line. In Gallinazo, if you can block the game while holding the lowest count, you force every opponent to take a full point hit.',
          tips: [
            'If you are the last player to move, be extremely careful. If you block the game and someone else has a tie or lower score, your 4x penalty can end your chances for the entire match.',
            "Pay attention to which suits (pip values) have been played 7 times. This tells you which ends are 'dead' and can be used to block.",
            'In the 7th deal, play extremely conservatively. This is when the secret scores are revealed and the pressure is highest.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
